History and fixes
Round 1 found four P2s: diagnostics/blank text counted as replies (R1-1), policy
lost by command matching (R1-2), dotted task names refused (R1-3), and tests
missing duplicate process launches (R1-4). Q1 asked for a task-specific NEW
fixer handoff destination; Q2 questioned large-policy performance. Firstmate
approved the four findings and Q1, leaving Q2 deferred. The first fix round
separated replies from diagnostics, tightened validation, reused task-name
validation, counted processes and generated a task-specific handoff destination.

Round 2 held those original cases except R1-2: full legacy command recognition
remained incomplete. It found R2-1: Claude success followed by an error object
was accepted as success. The captain approved a structural launch slot and
whole-response validation, red tests first. Both generated instruction paths
now preserve all policy in bounded fragments; only the filled launch slot
can direct launches. Existing slotless configurations work automatically.
No command recognition remains. Claude requires exactly one valid non-error
result object containing a meaningful string for both roles.

Round 3 held all prior fixes and found no new defect. Firstmate approved final
review, kept Q2 deferred and decided Q3 needed no live probe. The live-Claude
receipt and worker-interpretation limits remain accepted and unobserved.

Round 4 held earlier fixes, detected 26 mutations with zero survivors, and
found R4-1: exported CDPATH polluted directory captures for valid relative
paths. Firstmate approved a narrow launcher-local correction, red test first.
The fresh fixer recorded the cause before editing code, then demonstrated all
16 requested relative/default combinations failing on unchanged production.
The independent control passed while the two reported CDPATH cases failed.
The one-line correction unsets CDPATH before all launcher directory captures.
No parent environment or persistent coordination state changed.

Twenty new cases cover both roles/date-runtime branches and shadow-directory
lookup. Four focused mutations were detected: no correction, input-only,
output-only, and output suppression without neutralizing directory search.
All affected checks passed after exact restoration. The fix was committed,
then rebased onto advanced local main; all three affected tests and canonical
lint/doc checks passed again on the combined head before final review.

Round 5 independently held R4-1 and every earlier finding. It detected all
30 final red-gate mutations, with zero survivors and combined affected tests
passing after every restoration. It also found the new issue below.

R5-1 [P2]: bare relative filenames starting with a dash abort launch
Quoting a filename protects shell splitting but does not stop utilities from
interpreting it as an option. Input/output resolution passes bare relative
names to dirname and basename without an option terminator or normalization.
The same mechanism affects launcher lines 76, 78, 80 and 82.

Concrete example from the isolated task review directory:
  fm-review-round.sh reviewer --round 1 --in -round.md --out <fresh-absolute-output>
Observed: exit 1, dirname/basename report "illegal option", no role process.
Control --in ./-round.md succeeds with exactly one process.
A fresh --out -round.out similarly fails; --out ./-round.out succeeds.
Eight failing cases and eight controls cover both roles and both runtime dates.
Reproduction (NEW fake binaries; no real model call):
  python3 /Users/rymndcs/.codex/review-scratch/round5-kwz3d1bm/leading_dash.py

Likelihood: low-frequency manual filename edge case, deterministic when used.
Generated absolute paths are unaffected, and ./ is an available workaround.
No frequency was measured in real fleet use.
Who it hurts: a worker/operator using an otherwise valid bare relative name
inside the task review directory. Reviewer or fixer never starts, delaying work.
Impact: availability failure; nonzero result prevents false clean-review success.
No file destruction, outside-file write or false success was reproduced.

This is in the same path-processing area as the preceding fix, so the structural
option is explicit before another patch: normalize all accepted relative input
and output paths to safe absolute/dot-prefixed operands before utility parsing,
or consistently terminate option parsing for every path utility. Both must
cover input and output and preserve physical containment. Recommend a narrow
whole-primitive correction plus red tests for both roles/runtime branches,
leading-dash files/directories and controls; do not add unrelated path state.
Estimated correction/regression cost: 5-15 minutes. No fix was made on worker
authority at this boundary.

